NAME
  grep - Search for PATTERN in each FILE or standard input. PATTERN is, by
  default, a Lua regular expression (not POSIX/grep BRE/ERE syntax).
  Example: grep -i 'hello world' menu.lua main.lua

OPTIONS
Regexp selection and interpretation:
  -e PATTERN                use PATTERN as the pattern; repeat to match any
                            of several patterns. One per option argument
                            (-e PAT) or attached to the option (-ePAT).
                            With any -e/--regexp/--file given, the first
                            positional argument is a FILE, not the pattern.
      --regexp=PATTERN      same as -e PATTERN, long form
      --lua-regexp          negates -F (switch back to Lua regexp matching)
  -F, --fixed-strings       PATTERN is a plain string
      --file=FILE           use newline separated PATTERNs from FILE
  -w, --word-regexp         force PATTERN to match only whole words
  -x, --line-regexp         force PATTERN to match only whole lines
  -i, --ignore-case         ignore case distinctions

Miscellaneous:
      --label=LABEL         use LABEL instead of (standard input) for stdin prefix
  -s, --no-messages         suppress error messages
  -v, --invert-match        select non-matching lines
      --help                display help message and exit
  -V, --version             display help message and exit

Output control:
      --max-count=NUM       stop after NUM matches
  -n, --line-number         print line number with output lines
  -H, --with-filename       print the file name for each match
  -h, --no-filename         suppress the file name prefix on output
  -o, --only-matching       show only the part of a line matching PATTERN
  -q, --quiet, --silent     suppress all normal output
  -r, --recursive           search directories in FILEs recursively
  -L, --files-without-match print only names of FILEs containing no match
  -l, --files-with-matches  print only names of FILEs containing matches
  -c, --count               print only a count of matching lines per FILE
  -C, --color, --colour     user markers to highlight the matching strings
  -t, --trim                trim whitespace off results

DIVERGENCES FROM GNU grep
  - Lua patterns are used for matching, not POSIX regexps: use %d . + - *
    [sets] and Lua's (.)*() capture rules; anchors are ^ and $.
  - -C is short for --color/always here (GNU reserves -C for --context);
    --color is the portable spelling on this system.
  - -a means "also match within binary-looking lines" here, not GNU's
    --text passthrough.
  - Last of -h/-H rarely wins when both are given with -r; -h normally
    still suppresses names.
  - Case-insensitive matching rewrites patterns into character classes
    ([aA]); a pattern with a capture backreference (%1) may behave
    unexpectedly combined with -i.

When FILE is -, read stdin. With no FILE, read - or ./ if a command line -r.
If fewer than two FILEs are given, assume -h. Recursive searches that expand
to two or more files print the matching path as the file name. Exit status is
0 if any line is selected, 1 otherwise. If any error occurs and -q is not
given, the exit status is 2.
